  overlays: [
    { id: "secrets", label: "Secrets and configuration", nodes: ["ENV", "DC", "API", "WORKER", "WEB"], edges: ["28a", "28b", "28c", "28d"],
      facts: "One .env file on the host, read by Docker Compose, not committed to source control (Q52). The OpenAI model name is a config value (Q44). Render pool size, depth limit, politeness delay, and per-site intervals are configurable (Q19, Q21, Q24, Q29). Per-site values must live in the database; global defaults are likely in .env (inference). There is no vault, no rotation process, and no per-environment configuration, because there is one environment (Q12).",
      means: "Anyone with SSH to the host can read every secret. Anyone who can run docker inspect sees them in the container environment. A host backup that includes .env is a backup of every credential. Rotating the OpenAI key means editing the file and restarting the api and worker containers.",
      ask: "The full variable list; file owner and permissions; who has SSH; whether .env is in any backup; whether any secret appears in the compose file or Dockerfile; how a key rotation has been done so far." },
    { id: "auth", label: "API access and authorization", nodes: ["B", "RP", "API", "PG", "REDIS", "WEB"], edges: ["1", "4", "5", "5b", "6", "23"],
      facts: "Email and password sign-in with a session cookie (Q46). No SSO (Single Sign-On), SAML (Security Assertion Markup Language), OIDC (OpenID Connect), or MFA (Multi-Factor Authentication). Roles are admin and user (Q47). Site visibility comes from SiteAssignment rows with an optional category-visibility restriction, and compliance module data is visible only to admins and explicitly assigned users (Q47, Q50). Admins are created by an existing admin with no periodic review (Q48). The API is not exposed for third-party use (Q7), so there are no API keys or service accounts; every call is a user session.",
      means: "Authorization is enforced inside FastAPI on every request, by reading the session and then the user's role and SiteAssignment rows. Nothing in front of FastAPI (proxy or Next.js) enforces it. The worker has no user context; it acts with full database rights.",
      ask: "How a session is stored and expired; whether the cookie is marked HttpOnly, Secure, and SameSite; whether CSRF (Cross-Site Request Forgery, a browser tricked by another site into sending an authenticated request) protection exists, which matters because auth is a cookie; CORS (Cross-Origin Resource Sharing) settings; whether /docs is public; the password hashing algorithm; rate limiting on sign-in; whether admin actions such as role changes are logged (Q57 covers only review and ignore actions)." },
    { id: "deploy", label: "Build, deploy, and spin-up", nodes: ["ADO", "DEV", "DC", "ENV", "WEB", "API", "WORKER", "PG", "REDIS", "RP"], edges: ["29a", "29b", "28a", "28b", "28c", "28d"],
      facts: "Docker Compose, one container per service, one host (Q11, Q16). One environment for development, test, and production (Q12). Deploy is manual: SSH, pull, docker compose build, docker compose up (Q66). Tests run on demand. No CI (Continuous Integration, automated build and test on every change) and no CD (Continuous Delivery, automated deployment). Rollback is redeploying the previous code (Q67). The image is a few GB because it bundles Chromium, the JRE for LanguageTool, and Node for Lighthouse (Q13). Schema changes apply through create_all() and idempotent migrations in code (Q3), which run when the application starts (inference).",
      means: "Every deploy is a production deploy, with downtime while containers rebuild and restart. The only record of what is running is the git checkout on the host. A failed build can leave the site down. A migration bug surfaces in production on first start.",
      ask: "The exact deploy commands and how long a build takes; whether images are tagged with a commit or version; restart policies and health checks in the compose file; whether a deploy restarts the proxy; where the checkout lives on the host; who else has SSH; whether a second compose file for a test environment has been tried." },
    { id: "logging", label: "Logging and monitoring", nodes: ["WEB", "API", "WORKER", "REDIS", "PG", "BK"], edges: [],
      facts: "stdout container logs only, no central retention or review (Q54). No monitoring or alerting (Q68). Status is checked manually. The audit trail covers review and ignore actions (Q57).",
      means: "Logs sit in Docker's json-file driver on the host, readable with docker compose logs, and grow until rotation is configured (Docker does not rotate by default, inference). A worker crash, a failed backup, a skipped cron run, or an OpenAI outage is visible only to someone reading logs. arq writes a health-check key in Redis that could feed a simple monitor; nothing consumes it today.",
      ask: "Log driver and rotation settings; whether log lines are structured; which log lines mark scan start, page error, scan complete, and backup complete; whether Docker health checks are defined; what the developer looks at when checking status." }
  ],
