  overlaysTitle: "Decisions",
  overlaysIntro: "Nine design decisions, each tied to the boxes it affects. Pick one to light up its part of the map.",
  overlays: [
    { id: "d1", label: "4.1 The UI-to-API boundary", nodes: ["C", "RP", "WEB", "API"], edges: ["2", "4", "4b"],
      items: [
        "Pattern A: the proxy routes UI paths to Next.js and API paths (for example /api) to FastAPI; the browser calls both on one hostname; the session cookie set by FastAPI is sent automatically; Next.js holds no data logic. Choose it when the UI is mostly client-rendered, when other clients (scripts, mobile) will use the API, or when one auth path is preferred.",
        "Pattern B: Next.js server code calls FastAPI over the Compose network and forwards the session; the browser talks only to Next.js. Choose it when pages must be server-rendered with data, or when the API must not be reachable from browsers.",
        "Many systems use both: server-side fetches for the first render, client-side calls afterward. Write down which is the rule and which is the exception, because each pattern is a separate auth path to test." ] },
    { id: "d2", label: "4.2 Authentication and authorization", nodes: ["C", "API", "REDIS", "PG", "IDP"], edges: ["4", "6", "18"],
      items: [
        "Where enforced: inside FastAPI on every request. The proxy and Next.js do not authorize.",
        "Session or token: a session cookie for browser clients (mark it HttpOnly, Secure, SameSite); a bearer token only for non-browser clients. Cookie auth requires CSRF protection.",
        "Session store: a signed cookie keeps FastAPI stateless; Redis or PostgreSQL storage allows server-side revocation. Decide which matters more.",
        "Roles: keep them in PostgreSQL even with SSO; map provider groups to local roles; keep one local break-glass admin.",
        "Sign-in controls: rate limit the sign-in route; use a current password hash (argon2 or bcrypt); log role changes and admin actions, not only domain actions.",
        "SSO: plan the integration point at design time (OIDC or SAML library in FastAPI, redirect and callback routes, session issued locally) even if the first release uses passwords." ] },
    { id: "d3", label: "4.3 Synchronous request or background job", nodes: ["API", "REDIS", "WORKER", "PG", "C"], edges: ["7", "9", "10", "8", "17"],
      items: [
        "Move work into a job when any of these hold: expected duration above about one to two seconds; a call to an external service; a need for retries; use of a heavy runtime; a schedule; a result that several users will read later. Everything else stays in the request.",
        "1. The API validates the request, inserts a status row in PostgreSQL (job type, status queued, requester, timestamps), then enqueues with the row id as the argument and, when duplicates must be prevented, as the arq job id.",
        "2. The worker sets status running at start, writes progress at checkpoints, writes results to domain tables, and sets succeeded or failed with an error message at the end.",
        "3. The client polls a status endpoint (or subscribes over SSE) and reads results from domain tables when done.",
        "4. A retried or crashed job must be safe to run again: check state before acting, use upserts, and use idempotency keys with external providers that support them.",
        "5. Set job_timeout above the longest expected run, and add a sweeper (a cron job) that marks status rows stuck at running with no live job as failed." ] },
    { id: "d4", label: "4.4 Data", nodes: ["PG", "REDIS", "FILES", "BK"], edges: ["5", "10", "13", "19a", "19b"],
      items: [
        "PostgreSQL is the system of record. Every other store holds a copy, a cache, or a pointer.",
        "Schema changes through Alembic migrations, applied by an explicit command in the deploy, not implicitly at application start.",
        "Files outside the database: path and metadata in a table; the file on a volume or in an object store; both included in backups and in delete cascades; retention defined per file type.",
        "Retention: define per table and per file type before the first production scan or upload; growth is easier to measure from day one than to reconstruct.",
        "Backups: frequency from RPO; off-host destination; a restore test on a schedule; the backup job reports success and failure to logs and metrics.",
        "Redis contents: assume they can vanish; persistence on and on a volume only for queued jobs if losing them is unacceptable." ] },
    { id: "d5", label: "4.5 Configuration and secrets", nodes: ["ENV", "DC", "API", "WORKER", "WEB"], edges: ["21a", "21b", "21c", "21d"],
      items: [
        "Environment variables are the only way settings and secrets reach a container; nothing is read from files inside the image.",
        "One .env per environment, owned by a service account, mode 600, never committed; a base compose file plus an override per environment.",
        "Secret sources beyond .env, in order of effort: Docker secrets, a host-level secret store, a vault service. Move up when more than one person deploys or when rotation is required by policy.",
        "Rotation procedure written down: which variables, which containers restart, how the old value is revoked.",
        "Secrets are visible to anyone with Docker access on the host; treat host access as secret access." ] },
    { id: "d6", label: "4.6 Build, deploy, and environments", nodes: ["REPO", "OPS", "DC", "ENV", "WEB", "API", "WORKER"], edges: ["22", "23", "21b", "21c", "21d"],
      items: [
        "Images: one per service (web, api, worker; a separate heavy-worker image when runtimes are bundled). Tag every image with the commit or a version; the running version is then readable from the image tag.",
        "CI on every push: dependency install, tests, lint, image build, image and dependency vulnerability scan, push to a registry.",
        "CD: deploy by pulling tagged images and running compose up; rollback is compose up with the previous tag. Migrations run as an explicit step before the new API starts.",
        "Environments: development on the developer's machine; a test environment on a separate host or at least a separate compose project with its own database; production. The same images move through them.",
        "Health checks and restart policies on every service; the proxy sends traffic only to healthy containers.",
        "Downtime: with one host and Compose, a deploy restarts containers; state the acceptable window, or run two API containers and restart them one at a time." ] },
    { id: "d7", label: "4.7 Observability", nodes: ["LOGS", "WEB", "API", "WORKER", "REDIS", "BK"], edges: ["20a", "20b", "20c"],
      items: [
        "Logs: structured (JSON lines), with a request id that the API generates and passes to jobs, so one action can be traced across web, api, and worker. Rotation configured on the Docker daemon. Centralized when more than one person reads them.",
        "Metrics: queue depth and oldest job age; job duration and failure count by job type; request latency and error rate by route; database connection pool usage; disk and memory per container; backup success and age.",
        "Health: an API health endpoint that checks PostgreSQL and Redis; a consumer of arq's health-check key; container health checks in Compose.",
        "Alerts: stalled worker (oldest job age above a threshold), failed backup, disk above a threshold, error rate above a threshold, certificate expiry within 14 days." ] },
    { id: "d8", label: "4.8 Security baseline", nodes: ["RP", "API", "PG", "REDIS", "ENV", "X1", "X2"], edges: ["1", "4", "14", "12", "21a"],
      items: [
        "Only the proxy is published on the host; PostgreSQL, Redis, and the application ports stay on the Compose network.",
        "TLS with a managed certificate and renewal; HTTP redirected to HTTPS; security headers at the proxy.",
        "Cookie flags, CSRF protection, CORS restricted to known origins, rate limiting on sign-in and expensive routes.",
        "A least-privilege database user for the application; a separate user for backups and migrations if practical.",
        "Dependency and image scanning in CI; a patching cadence written down.",
        "Egress: a list of the destinations the system needs (including everything a headless browser will fetch) and a record of what data leaves to each.",
        "Audit: who changed what, when, for both domain actions and administrative actions; exportable." ] },
    { id: "d9", label: "4.9 Capacity on a single host", nodes: ["WORKER", "RT", "FILES", "PG", "REDIS", "LOGS"], edges: ["11", "13", "5", "10", "9"],
      items: [
        "What runs out first on this stack, and the metric that shows it:",
        "1. Memory, from headless browsers and Java runtimes in the worker: memory per container, out-of-memory kills in the kernel log.",
        "2. Disk, from files, logs, and backup dumps stored locally: disk usage and growth per day.",
        "3. Database connections, from API processes times pool size plus worker processes times pool size: active connections versus max_connections.",
        "4. CPU, from rendering and scoring: worker CPU and job duration.",
        "5. Queue latency, when jobs arrive faster than the worker finishes them: queue depth and oldest job age." ] }
  ],
