  proxy: {
    why: "Two servers (Next.js and FastAPI) have to look like one site on one hostname, TLS has to terminate somewhere, and neither Node nor uvicorn should be the process exposed to the network. A reverse proxy solves all three: it is the single published listener, it holds the certificate, and it routes by path to whichever backend owns that path. Without it the browser would need two hostnames (which breaks cookie sharing and brings in CORS), each backend would need its own certificate, and each would take unfiltered traffic directly.",
    how: "The proxy listens on 443, completes the TLS handshake with the browser, and reads the request. A routing table decides where it goes: UI paths to web:3000, API paths to api:8000, over plain HTTP on the private Compose network. It adds headers that tell the backend the original client address and scheme (X-Forwarded-For, X-Forwarded-Proto), relays the response, and applies limits: request body size, read timeout, connection counts. When the backend is down it answers 502; when the backend is too slow it answers 504. It can also serve static files, add security headers, and rate limit.",
    setup: "In this stack it is either a container in the Compose file (nginx, Caddy, or Traefik, with the certificate mounted or fetched automatically) or nginx installed on the host with a site configuration that points at published container ports. The pieces to find: the config file with the routing rules, the certificate and key paths and their renewal method, the timeout and body-size values (nginx defaults are a 60 second read timeout and a 1 MB body limit), and whether the proxy is restarted by compose up. If Traefik or Caddy is used, routing may be declared as labels on the other services instead of in one file.",
    inspect: [
