  fastapi: {
    why: "The application needs one place where every request is authenticated, validated, authorized, and turned into database work or a queued job. Putting that in a Python API keeps business rules next to the crawling code (Playwright, axe integration, SQLAlchemy are all Python) and separate from the UI, so the UI can change without touching the rules and other clients could use the same API later. FastAPI in particular gives typed request and response models, asynchronous handlers, and self-generated OpenAPI documentation.",
    how: "An ASGI server (uvicorn) accepts HTTP on 8000 and hands each request to FastAPI. FastAPI matches the path to a route function, runs its dependencies (reading the session cookie and loading the user is one of them), validates the body against a Pydantic model (422 if it does not fit), runs the handler, and serializes the result to JSON. Handlers are async: while one waits on PostgreSQL or Redis, the same process serves other requests. A blocking call inside a handler (a synchronous HTTP call to OpenAI, a long computation) stalls every other request in that process until it returns, which is why long work goes to the worker.",
