  redis: {
    why: "The API must hand a scan to the worker without calling it directly, and the worker must be able to be down, busy, or restarted without the API noticing. A queue in a fast shared store does that: the API writes a job and returns; the worker takes jobs when it can. Redis is used because arq is built on it and because it answers in well under a millisecond, which also makes it the usual home for sessions, caches, and rate-limit counters.",
    how: "Redis keeps all data in memory and processes commands one at a time on a single thread, which is why it is fast and why nothing in it should be expensive. arq stores the queue as a sorted set named arq:queue (job ids ordered by scheduled time), each job's function name and arguments under arq:job:<id>, results under arq:result:<id> for keep_result seconds (3600 by default), and a health-check key the worker refreshes. Persistence is optional: RDB writes periodic snapshots, AOF appends every write; with neither, a restart empties the queue.",
    setup: "The redis image with a volume on /data and a command line or redis.conf that turns persistence on if queued jobs must survive a restart. Inside the Compose network it usually runs without a password, which is acceptable only because port 6379 is not published on the host; confirm that. The API and worker share one REDIS_URL from the environment.",
    inspect: [
      ["docker compose exec redis redis-cli INFO persistence", "aof_enabled and rdb_last_save_time: whether anything survives a restart"],
      ["docker compose exec redis redis-cli ZCARD arq:queue", "jobs waiting; a number that only grows means the worker is not consuming"],
      ["docker compose exec redis redis-cli --scan --pattern 'arq:*' | head -50", "the job, result, and health keys present right now"],
      ["docker compose exec redis redis-cli INFO memory", "memory in use; Redis holds everything in RAM"],
      ["docker compose config | grep -n 6379", "whether 6379 is published on the host (it should not be)"]
    ],
    terms: [["Broker", "the store a queue lives in"], ["Sorted set", "the Redis structure arq uses for the queue"], ["RDB and AOF", "snapshot persistence and append-only persistence"], ["TTL", "time to live; keys such as results expire on their own"], ["Eviction", "what Redis does when memory is full; the policy decides what is dropped"]],
    resources: [
      { label: "Redis documentation", url: "https://redis.io/docs/" },
      { label: "Redis persistence", url: "https://redis.io/docs/latest/operate/oss_and_stack/management/persistence/" },
      { label: "ByteByteGo posts PDF, p. 76, caching" }
    ]
  },
  arqworker: {
    why: "A scan runs for minutes to hours, needs a browser and a Java process, must retry on transient failures, and must not hold an HTTP request open. That work belongs in a separate process that takes jobs from a queue: it can be restarted, given more memory, or run on another machine without changing the API, and a crash in a scan cannot take the API down with it.",
    how: "The worker is a Python process started from an arq WorkerSettings class that lists the job functions. It polls Redis every 0.5 seconds, claims up to max_jobs jobs (default 10) and runs each as a coroutine. A job is cancelled when it exceeds job_timeout (default 300 seconds) and retried up to max_tries (default 5). arq uses pessimistic execution: a job stays in the queue until it finishes or fails, so a worker that is killed mid-job leaves the job to run again when a worker returns. That means every job must be safe to run twice. The arq project describes itself as in maintenance-only mode, which matters for a long-lived system.",
    setup: "A Compose service, usually from the same image as the API, whose command is arq followed by the import path of WorkerSettings. It needs the same REDIS_URL and database settings, plus Chromium, the JRE, and Node in its image for the scan pipeline. Memory is the setting to decide on purpose: a memory limit in Compose and a render pool size that fits inside it. A single worker process is what Q6 describes; a second process is a second Compose replica.",
    inspect: [
      ["docker compose logs -f worker", "job start and completion lines with durations, tracebacks, and arq's own retry and timeout messages"],
      ["docker compose exec worker arq --check APP.MODULE.WorkerSettings", "arq's built-in health check against the running settings (substitute the real import path)"],
      ["docker compose exec redis redis-cli GET arq:queue:health-check", "the health string the worker refreshes: jobs complete, failed, retried, ongoing"],
      ["docker stats --no-stream", "memory and CPU per container; the worker is the one that grows"],
      ["sudo dmesg -T | grep -i -E 'killed process|out of memory' | tail", "whether the kernel has been killing Chromium or the worker for memory"]
    ],
    terms: [["Job", "one queued unit of work: function name plus arguments"], ["At-least-once", "a job can run more than once; the code must tolerate it"], ["Idempotent", "safe to repeat with the same result"], ["job_timeout, max_jobs, max_tries", "arq's per-job limit, concurrency, and retry count"], ["Backpressure", "what happens when jobs arrive faster than they finish: the queue grows"]],
    resources: [
      { label: "arq documentation", url: "https://arq-docs.helpmanual.io/" },
      { label: "arq repository (notes the maintenance-only status)", url: "https://github.com/python-arq/arq" },
      { label: "ByteByteGo posts PDF, p. 244, retries; p. 278, idempotency; p. 98, polling versus webhooks" }
    ]
  },
  arqcron: {
    why: "The daily recrawl check has to start without anyone clicking. Running the schedule inside the worker avoids a separate scheduler process and gives the scheduled job the same runtime the scans use.",
    how: "cron entries declared in WorkerSettings name a job function and the minutes, hours, or weekdays it runs. When the worker's clock reaches a matching time it enqueues the job with an id derived from that time, so two workers do not run the same tick twice. A tick that falls while no worker is running is skipped, not caught up. The clock is the container's, and Docker containers run in UTC unless TZ is set, so 'daily at 02:00' means 02:00 UTC, which is 20:00 or 21:00 in Lubbock depending on daylight saving time.",
    setup: "The schedule is code, not configuration: find the cron() entries in the worker settings module. The per-site interval from Q29 is data in PostgreSQL that the scheduled job reads. Check the TZ variable for the worker container, and whether run_at_startup is set (which would run the job every time the worker restarts).",
