  playwright: {
    why: "Campus pages assemble themselves with JavaScript after the HTML arrives, so fetching the HTML alone would miss menus, widgets, and errors that only exist in a real browser. axe-core needs the live page to test. Playwright is the automation library that drives a headless Chromium, and it is also how the crawler follows links, reads sitemap.xml and robots.txt, and takes the screenshots reviewers see.",
    how: "The Python package starts a Node driver process that controls Chromium over the Chrome DevTools Protocol. One browser hosts several contexts (isolated sessions with their own cookies) and each context opens pages. For each URL the worker navigates, waits for the page to settle, runs scripts in the page (that is where axe-core is injected), reads text and the final URL, and captures a screenshot as PNG bytes. Each context costs memory (hundreds of MB is normal), so the render pool caps how many pages are open at once, and the politeness delay spaces requests to the same site.",
    setup: "The image needs Chromium and its system libraries: either Microsoft's Playwright base image or a Debian image with playwright install chromium --with-deps. Two container settings matter: memory (a limit that fits pool size times per-context cost) and /dev/shm, which Docker sizes at 64 MB by default and Chromium uses heavily; the fix is shm_size in the Compose file or launching with --disable-dev-shm-usage. Per-page timeouts, the pool size, and the user agent are settings in the crawler code or environment.",
    inspect: [
