  axe: {
    why: "The accessibility rules have to be applied the same way to every page, every time, without a person reading each one. axe-core is the open-source rules engine most scanners and browser tools are built on, so its results are comparable with what a developer sees in their own browser extension. Automated rules cover only the criteria a machine can decide; the rest is what the incomplete bucket and manual review are for.",
    how: "The worker injects the axe-core script into the rendered page and calls it. Each rule selects elements and runs checks against the live DOM and computed styles: image alt text present, color contrast ratio, form labels, heading order, ARIA attributes valid. The output is JSON with four lists: violations, passes, incomplete (axe could not decide, for example contrast over a background image), and inapplicable. Each item carries the rule id, an impact level, WCAG tags such as wcag2a or wcag22aa, and the affected elements with CSS selectors and HTML snippets. Which rules run is a configuration (tags or an explicit list), and the rule set changes between axe versions.",
    setup: "axe-core is a JavaScript file bundled into the image (directly, or through a Python helper package) and injected per page. The configuration to find: the axe version pin, the tags or rules selected (Q5 says A, AA, and AAA), and how the result maps to the check catalog and scoring config (Q34, Q35). A site's Content Security Policy can block injected scripts, which shows up as a page with no findings at all.",
    inspect: [
