  files: {
    why: "Screenshots are images; database rows are for facts about them. Storing binaries on disk and the path in the database keeps the database small and fast and lets a web server hand the files out directly. The cost is that files live outside the transaction and the backup unless someone includes them on purpose.",
    how: "The worker writes a file to a directory inside its container; the directory is mounted from a Docker volume or a host path so the file outlives the container. The Page row keeps the path or name (inference). Whatever serves the image (FastAPI or Next.js) must mount the same directory. Deleting a site removes rows, not files, unless the code does both, which is where orphaned files come from (Q63).",
