    how: "pg_dump asks PostgreSQL for a consistent copy of the database while it runs and writes it as SQL or in a custom format that pg_restore reads; it does not need to stop anything. File copies (screenshots, .env) are separate. A dump on the same disk as the database protects against a bad migration but not against a failed disk or a lost host, which is why copies go somewhere else. A backup that has never been restored is an assumption; Q59 says a restore has been tested once.",
    setup: "A dump after each scan, triggered by the worker or a script (Q59). The pieces to find: the exact command and format, the destination directory, whether copies leave the host, retention, whether screenshots and .env are included, and the restore procedure written down as commands. A scheduled restore into a scratch database is the check that keeps it real.",
