  compose: {
    why: "Five or six processes with different runtimes have to start together, find each other, keep their data, and be rebuilt the same way every time. Docker Compose describes all of that in one file, so the whole system is reproducible from the file plus .env, and the network between services is private by default.",
    how: "docker compose up reads compose.yaml, substitutes variables from .env, builds images from Dockerfiles where needed, creates a private network where each service is reachable by its name (db, redis, api), creates volumes, and starts containers in dependency order. Each service gets its environment, mounts, published ports, restart policy, and optional health check from the file. docker compose logs reads every container's stdout. Rebuilding replaces containers; volumes survive.",
    setup: "One compose file (plus an override file per environment when there is more than one). The entries that decide behavior in an outage: restart policy (whether a crashed worker comes back), healthcheck (whether the proxy can tell the API is up), depends_on (start order), mem_limit or deploy resources, logging options. The published ports list is the security boundary: only the proxy should appear in it.",
