  llm: {
    why: "Explaining an accessibility issue in plain language and summarizing what changed on a page are text tasks a language model does well, and running a capable model locally would need hardware the server does not have (Q39). A hosted API trades that for cost per call, a network dependency, and data leaving campus.",
    how: "The application sends an HTTPS POST to api.openai.com with a bearer API key, the model name, and a prompt that includes the issue and page text; the response is generated text billed by tokens in and out. Calls take seconds, can be rate limited (429), and fail if the key is invalid (401) or egress is blocked. The response is shown as advisory text (Q43) and not stored (Q45), so each click pays again (Q15).",
    setup: "OPENAI_API_KEY and the model name in .env (Q44), the OpenAI Python SDK in the image, and egress to api.openai.com. Decisions to record: which process makes each call (API for Ask AI, worker for summaries by inference), timeouts, what text is sent, the account's data-use settings, and a monthly spend ceiling.",
    inspect: [
      ["the OpenAI platform usage page for the account", "spend and call volume; the only place cost is visible"],
      ["docker compose logs api | grep -i -E 'openai|429|401'", "failed calls and their reason"],
      ["docker compose exec api python -c 'import openai; print(openai.__version__)'", "the SDK version"],
      ["docker compose exec api sh -c 'wget -q -S -O /dev/null https://api.openai.com/v1/models 2>&1 | head -1'", "egress check: an HTTP 401 without a key proves the host can reach OpenAI; a hang or DNS error proves it cannot"]
    ],
    terms: [["Token", "the unit of text the API bills"], ["Prompt", "the text sent to the model, including the page text"], ["Model name", "which model answers; a config value"], ["Rate limit", "calls or tokens per minute the account may use"], ["Data residency", "where sent text is processed and stored"]],
    resources: [
      { label: "OpenAI platform documentation", url: "https://platform.openai.com/docs/" }
    ]
  },
  sms: {
    why: "Site owners are not in the Compass UI when a page changes; a text message reaches them anywhere. Sending SMS reliably means using a carrier-connected service rather than building one, which is what Twilio provides (Q74).",
    how: "The application sends an HTTPS POST to Twilio's Messages endpoint with the account SID and auth token, a sending number, a destination number, and the text. Twilio queues it, delivers through carriers, and can call back with delivery status. Each message segment costs money. In the United States, application-to-person traffic from standard numbers requires campaign registration (A2P 10DLC) before carriers deliver at volume.",
    setup: "Credentials and the sending number in .env, the Twilio SDK or plain HTTPS in the worker, and egress to api.twilio.com. Decisions to record: who owns the account and pays, which process sends, how numbers are collected and verified, and what happens on a failed send.",
    inspect: [
      ["the Twilio console message logs", "sent, delivered, and failed messages with error codes"],
      ["docker compose logs worker | grep -i twilio", "send attempts and errors from the application side"],
      ["cut -d= -f1 PROJECTDIR/.env | grep -i twilio", "which Twilio settings exist, without values"]
    ],
    terms: [["Account SID and auth token", "Twilio's account identifier and secret"], ["Segment", "a 160-character unit of billing"], ["A2P 10DLC", "US carrier registration for application traffic on standard numbers"], ["Status callback", "Twilio calling the application back with delivery results"]],
    resources: [
      { label: "Twilio messaging documentation", url: "https://www.twilio.com/docs/messaging" }
    ]
  },
  repo: {
    why: "Code needs a history, a place to review changes, and a single source of truth for what should be running. A hosted git service provides that, and it is where an automated pipeline (build, test, scan, publish images) would run once one exists (Q66 says none does).",
    how: "Developers commit to branches and merge through pull requests. A pipeline, when configured, runs on every push: install dependencies, run tests, build images, tag them with the commit, and push them to a registry, so a deploy becomes 'pull this tag' rather than 'build on the server'. Without it, the server's checkout is the only record of what is live.",
    setup: "The repository TTUOnline/Development/Website_Compliance_Tool in Azure DevOps (Q10). A first pipeline is one YAML file that runs the test suite and builds the images; a second step publishes tagged images to a registry the server can pull from.",
    inspect: [
      ["git -C PROJECTDIR log -1 --oneline", "the commit that is actually running on the server"],
      ["git -C PROJECTDIR status --short", "changes on the server that were never committed (drift)"],
      ["git -C PROJECTDIR remote -v", "which repository the server pulls from"],
      ["the repository's Pipelines page in Azure DevOps", "whether any pipeline exists and when it last ran"]
    ],
    terms: [["Commit", "one recorded change with an id"], ["Tag", "a name for a commit, used to label images"], ["Pull request", "a reviewed change before merge"], ["CI", "automated build and test on every change"], ["CD", "automated deployment of what CI produced"], ["Registry", "where built images are stored"]],
    resources: [
      { label: "Azure DevOps documentation", url: "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/devops/" },
      { label: "Azure Pipelines documentation", url: "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/devops/pipelines/" },
      { label: "ByteByteGo posts PDF, p. 105, shipping code to production; p. 197, DevSecOps" }
    ]
  },
  workstation: {
    why: "Something has to run the deploy on the host. Today that is a person with SSH access and Docker rights (Q66), which makes that person part of the architecture: their key, their knowledge of the commands, and their availability are dependencies (Q64, Q69).",
    how: "The developer connects over SSH with a key, pulls the repository on the server, and runs docker compose build and docker compose up, which rebuild images and replace containers. Membership in the docker group grants the same power as root on the host, including reading every secret in every container's environment.",
    setup: "An authorized SSH key on the server, membership in the docker group or sudo, and a written runbook with the exact commands for deploy, rollback, restart, and restore. The runbook is what turns one person's knowledge into something a second person can execute.",
    inspect: [
      ["sudo cat /home/*/.ssh/authorized_keys /root/.ssh/authorized_keys 2>/dev/null", "who can log in"],
      ["getent group docker; sudo -l -U USERNAME", "who has Docker or sudo rights"],
      ["last -n 20", "recent logins"],
      ["sudo journalctl -u ssh --since '14 days ago' | grep -i accepted | tail", "accepted SSH logins with user and source (unit name may be sshd)"]
    ],
    terms: [["SSH key", "the credential that grants shell access"], ["docker group", "membership equals root-level access to the host"], ["Runbook", "written step-by-step operating procedures"], ["Bus factor", "how many people can be unavailable before work stops; here, one"]],
    resources: [
      { label: "OpenSSH manual pages", url: "https://www.openssh.com/manual.html" },
      { label: "docker compose command reference", url: "https://docs.docker.com/reference/cli/docker/compose/" }
    ]
  },
  logs: {
    why: "When something fails at 3 a.m., the log lines are the only evidence of what happened, and a metric is the only way to notice before a user does. Without collection, rotation, and a few alerts, the system runs but nobody can tell what it is doing, which is the state Q54 and Q68 describe.",
    how: "Every container writes to stdout; the Docker daemon's log driver stores it (json-file by default, one file per container under /var/lib/docker/containers) and docker compose logs reads it. Docker does not rotate json-file logs unless told to, so they grow until the disk is full. Centralizing means shipping those files to a collector (Loki, Elastic, a cloud service) and adding a request id so one action can be followed across web, api, and worker. Metrics come from exporters or the application itself and feed alerts.",
    setup: "Log rotation in /etc/docker/daemon.json (log-opts max-size and max-file, or the local driver), structured JSON logging in the application, a health endpoint on the API, a consumer of arq's health key, and alerts for the failures that stay silent otherwise: stalled worker, failed backup, full disk, certificate expiry.",
    inspect: [
      ["docker inspect --format '{{.LogPath}}' CONTAINER", "where a container's log file lives"],
      ["sudo du -sh /var/lib/docker/containers/*/", "how big each container's log has grown"],
      ["cat /etc/docker/daemon.json", "whether rotation or a different driver is configured"],
      ["docker compose logs --since 24h worker | grep -i -E 'error|traceback' | tail", "yesterday's failures nobody was told about"]
    ],
    terms: [["Log driver", "the Docker component that stores container output"], ["Rotation", "capping log size and keeping a fixed number of files"], ["Structured logging", "log lines as JSON fields rather than free text"], ["Request id", "one identifier carried through every log line of one action"], ["Alert", "a rule that pages a person when a metric crosses a threshold"]],
    resources: [
      { label: "Docker, configure logging drivers", url: "https://docs.docker.com/engine/logging/configure/" },
      { label: "ByteByteGo posts PDF, p. 330, fault-tolerant systems" }
    ]
  },
  idp: {
    why: "Institutional accounts already carry identity, MFA, and deprovisioning. Signing in through the identity provider means Compass stops holding passwords, an offboarded person loses access when their campus account closes, and MFA comes for free. The worksheet says none of this exists yet (Q46).",
    how: "With OIDC or SAML, the application redirects the browser to the provider, the user signs in there, and the provider sends the browser back with a signed assertion or token. The application (FastAPI is the relying party) verifies it, looks up or creates the local user, maps provider groups to local roles, and issues its own session cookie as before. Local roles still live in PostgreSQL; a local break-glass admin remains for provider outages.",
    setup: "Register the application with the provider (client id, secret, callback URL), add the redirect and callback routes in FastAPI with an OIDC or SAML library, keep the session mechanism, and decide the group-to-role mapping. Plan the integration point at design time even if passwords ship first.",
    inspect: [
      ["the provider's application registration", "client id, callback URL, allowed groups"],
      ["docker compose logs api | grep -i -E 'saml|oidc|callback'", "sign-in failures and their reasons"]
    ],
    terms: [["OIDC", "OpenID Connect, sign-in built on OAuth 2.0"], ["SAML", "the older XML-based single sign-on standard common in universities"], ["Relying party", "the application that trusts the provider"], ["Break-glass account", "a local admin that works when the provider does not"]],
    resources: [
      { label: "OpenID Foundation, how OpenID Connect works", url: "https://openid.net/developers/how-connect-works/" },
      { label: "ByteByteGo posts PDF, p. 270, SSO; p. 88, session, cookie, JWT, SSO" }
    ]
  },
  runtimes: {
    why: "Python cannot render a web page, run a Java grammar engine, or execute a Node audit tool by itself. Those capabilities come as separate runtimes that live inside the worker image and are started as child processes. They are the reason the worker image is large and the reason the worker is the component that runs out of memory first.",
    how: "The worker launches the runtime (a headless browser through a driver, a Java server on a local port, a Node program on the command line), talks to it over a local pipe or HTTP, and reads results back. Each has its own memory footprint and startup time, and a crash inside one can take the parent process with it if memory is exhausted.",
    setup: "Install the runtime in the Dockerfile, give the container a memory limit that fits the expected concurrency, size /dev/shm for a browser, and cap how many instances run at once. When the image or the memory profile diverges from the light jobs, split the heavy jobs into their own worker image and queue (growth step 5).",
    inspect: [
      ["docker stats --no-stream", "memory per container while jobs run"],
      ["docker compose exec worker df -h /dev/shm", "shared memory available to a browser"],
      ["sudo dmesg -T | grep -i -E 'killed process|out of memory' | tail", "kernel memory kills"],
      ["docker images", "image sizes; a multi-GB worker image is the runtimes"]
    ],
    terms: [["Child process", "a program the worker starts and controls"], ["/dev/shm", "shared memory; Chromium needs more than Docker's 64 MB default"], ["OOM kill", "the kernel ending a process to free memory"]],
    resources: [
      { label: "Playwright, running in Docker", url: "https://playwright.dev/python/docs/docker" },
      { label: "Chrome headless mode", url: "https://developer.chrome.com/docs/chromium/headless" }
    ]
  },
  thirdparty: {
    why: "Some capabilities are cheaper and better to rent than to build: language models, SMS, email delivery, payments. Each one adds a network dependency, a credential, a bill, and a flow of data out of the organization, so each needs the same four decisions: timeout and retry, cost ceiling, what leaves, and behavior when it is down.",
    how: "The application calls an HTTPS endpoint with an API key or OAuth token, waits for a response, and handles failures: 401 (bad key), 429 (rate limited), 5xx and timeouts. Calls made from the worker can retry; calls made inside an API request hold the user waiting, so they must be short and bounded. Idempotency keys, where the provider supports them, make retries safe.",
    setup: "Credentials in the environment only, the provider's SDK or plain HTTPS, explicit timeouts, egress rules for the provider's hostnames, and a record of which data is sent to whom and under what agreement.",
    inspect: [
      ["the provider's dashboard", "usage, spend, and error rates from their side"],
      ["docker compose logs worker | grep -i -E '401|429|timeout'", "failures from the application side"],
      ["cut -d= -f1 PROJECTDIR/.env", "which provider credentials exist"]
    ],
    terms: [["API key", "a secret that identifies the account"], ["Rate limit", "calls allowed per period"], ["Idempotency key", "a token that makes a retried request safe to repeat"], ["Data processing agreement", "the contract covering data sent to the provider"]],
    resources: [
      { label: "ByteByteGo posts PDF, p. 244, retries; p. 278, idempotency" }
    ]
  }
};

window.CODE = [{"path": "compose.yaml", "desc": "One file describes the whole system. Service names are hostnames on the private network.", "regions": [{"tags": ["DC"], "code": "# docker compose up reads this file plus .env, creates the private network,\n# and starts every service. Only the proxy publishes a port on the host.\nservices:"}, {"tags": ["RP", "1", "2", "4"], "code": "  proxy:                                   # the only published listener\n    image: nginx:1.27\n    ports: [\"443:443\", \"80:80\"]\n    volumes:\n      - ./proxy/nginx.conf:/etc/nginx/conf.d/default.conf:ro\n      - ./proxy/certs:/etc/nginx/certs:ro\n    depends_on: [web, api]\n    restart: unless-stopped"}, {"tags": ["WEB", "2", "3", "21d"], "code": "  web:                                     # the UI server\n    build: ./web\n    env_file: .env\n    expose: [\"3000\"]                       # reachable by the proxy, not from the host\n    restart: unless-stopped\n    logging: &log                          # rotation, because Docker does not rotate by default\n      driver: json-file\n      options: { max-size: \"20m\", max-file: \"5\" }"}, {"tags": ["API", "4", "5", "7", "21b", "d7"], "code": "  api:\n    build: ./api\n    command: uvicorn app.main:app --host 0.0.0.0 --port 8000 --workers 2\n    env_file: .env\n    expose: [\"8000\"]\n    depends_on:\n      db: { condition: service_healthy }\n      redis: { condition: service_healthy }\n    healthcheck:                           # the proxy and a monitor can tell the API is up\n      test: [\"CMD\", \"python\", \"-c\", \"import urllib.request; urllib.request.urlopen('http://localhost:8000/health')\"]\n      interval: 30s\n      timeout: 5s\n      retries: 3\n    restart: unless-stopped\n    logging: *log"}, {"tags": ["WORKER", "RT", "FILES", "9", "11", "13", "21c", "d9"], "code": "  worker:\n    build: { context: ., dockerfile: worker/Dockerfile }\n    command: arq worker.WorkerSettings\n    env_file: .env\n    depends_on: [db, redis]\n    shm_size: 1g                           # Chromium needs more than Docker's 64 MB default\n    mem_limit: 4g                          # memory, not CPU, is what runs out first\n    volumes:\n      - files:/data/files                  # binaries live here; rows keep the path\n    restart: unless-stopped\n    logging: *log"}, {"tags": ["PG", "5", "10", "d4", "d8"], "code": "  db:\n    image: postgres:16\n    env_file: .env\n    volumes:\n      - pgdata:/var/lib/postgresql/data    # the data directory survives rebuilds\n    healthcheck:\n      test: [\"CMD-SHELL\", \"pg_isready -U ${POSTGRES_USER} -d ${POSTGRES_DB}\"]\n      interval: 10s\n      timeout: 5s\n      retries: 5\n    restart: unless-stopped\n    # no ports entry: reachable only on the Compose network"}, {"tags": ["REDIS", "6", "7", "9", "d4"], "code": "  redis:\n    image: redis:7\n    command: [\"redis-server\", \"--appendonly\", \"yes\"]   # queued jobs survive a restart\n    volumes:\n      - redisdata:/data\n    healthcheck:\n      test: [\"CMD\", \"redis-cli\", \"ping\"]\n      interval: 10s\n      timeout: 3s\n      retries: 5\n    restart: unless-stopped"}, {"tags": ["FILES", "PG", "REDIS", "d4"], "code": "volumes:\n  pgdata:\n  redisdata:\n  files:"}]}, {"path": ".env.example", "desc": "Copy to .env on the host, chmod 600, never commit. Every setting a container reads.", "regions": [{"tags": ["ENV", "21a", "d5"], "code": "# Compose substitutes ${NAME} in compose.yaml from this file and passes\n# the whole file into containers with env_file. Anyone with Docker access\n# on the host can read these values."}, {"tags": ["ENV", "PG"], "code": "POSTGRES_USER=app\nPOSTGRES_PASSWORD=change-me\nPOSTGRES_DB=app\nDATABASE_URL=postgresql+asyncpg://app:change-me@db:5432/app"}, {"tags": ["ENV", "REDIS"], "code": "REDIS_URL=redis://redis:6379/0"}, {"tags": ["ENV", "API", "d2"], "code": "SESSION_SECRET=change-me-32-random-bytes      # rotate it to sign everyone out\nPUBLIC_BASE_URL=https://example.org\nAPI_INTERNAL_URL=http://api:8000              # Pattern B only: server-side calls from web to api"}, {"tags": ["ENV", "X1", "14", "15"], "code": "LLM_API_KEY=\nLLM_MODEL=your-model-name\nSMS_ACCOUNT_SID=\nSMS_AUTH_TOKEN="}, {"tags": ["ENV", "WORKER", "RT", "CRON", "d9"], "code": "JOB_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=3600                      # above the longest expected job\nMAX_JOBS=4                                    # concurrent jobs per worker process\nRENDER_POOL=3                                 # browser contexts open at once; sized by memory\nPOLITENESS_DELAY_SECONDS=1.0\nTZ=America/Chicago                            # containers default to UTC; cron uses this clock"}]}, {"path": "proxy/nginx.conf", "desc": "TLS terminates here. Backends speak plain HTTP on the Compose network.", "regions": [{"tags": ["RP", "1", "d8"], "code": "server {\n    listen 80;\n    return 301 https://$host$request_uri;          # plain HTTP only redirects\n}\nserver {\n    listen 443 ssl;\n    server_name example.org;\n    ssl_certificate     /etc/nginx/certs/fullchain.pem;\n    ssl_certificate_key /etc/nginx/certs/privkey.pem;\n    add_header Strict-Transport-Security \"max-age=31536000\" always;\n    client_max_body_size 20m;                      # nginx default is 1m\n    proxy_read_timeout 60s;                        # anything longer than this is a job, not a request"}, {"tags": ["RP", "API", "4", "17", "d1"], "code": "    location /api/ {                               # Pattern A: the browser calls the API on this hostname\n        proxy_pass http://api:8000/;               # trailing slash strips the /api prefix\n        proxy_set_header Host $host;\n        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for;\n        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Proto https;\n    }"}, {"tags": ["RP", "WEB", "2", "3"], "code": "    location / {                                   # everything else is the UI server\n        proxy_pass http://web:3000;\n        proxy_set_header Host $host;\n        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for;\n    }\n}"}]}, {"path": "api/app/settings.py", "desc": "Settings arrive as environment variables. Missing values fail at start, not on first use.", "regions": [{"tags": ["API", "ENV", "21b", "d5"], "code": "from pydantic_settings import BaseSettings\n\nclass Settings(BaseSettings):\n    database_url: str\n    redis_url: str\n    session_secret: str\n    public_base_url: str = \"http://localhost\"\n    job_timeout_seconds: int = 3600\n    politeness_delay_seconds: float = 1.0\n    llm_api_key: str = \"\"\n    llm_model: str = \"\"\n    oidc_discovery_url: str = \"\"    # SSO, optional\n    oidc_client_id: str = \"\"\n    oidc_client_secret: str = \"\"\n\nsettings = Settings()   # reads the container environment once, at import"}]}, {"path": "api/app/db.py", "desc": "One engine and one connection pool per process.", "regions": [{"tags": ["API", "PG", "5", "d9"], "code": "from sqlalchemy.ext.asyncio import create_async_engine, async_sessionmaker\nfrom .settings import settings\n\n# 2 uvicorn workers x (pool_size + max_overflow) = at most 20 API connections;\n# add the worker's pool and stay under PostgreSQL's max_connections (100).\nengine = create_async_engine(settings.database_url, pool_size=5, max_overflow=5)\nSessionLocal = async_sessionmaker(engine, expire_on_commit=False)\n\nasync def get_session():\n    async with SessionLocal() as session:\n        yield session"}]}, {"path": "api/app/models.py", "desc": "The system of record. The Job table is the status row users see progress from.", "regions": [{"tags": ["PG", "d4"], "code": "from datetime import datetime\nfrom sqlalchemy import DateTime, ForeignKey, Integer, String, Text, UniqueConstraint, func\nfrom sqlalchemy.dialects.postgresql import JSONB\nfrom sqlalchemy.orm import DeclarativeBase, Mapped, mapped_column\n\nclass Base(DeclarativeBase):\n    pass"}, {"tags": ["PG", "API", "d2"], "code": "class User(Base):\n    __tablename__ = \"users\"\n    id: Mapped[int] = mapped_column(primary_key=True)\n    email: Mapped[str] = mapped_column(String(255), unique=True)\n    password_hash: Mapped[str] = mapped_column(String(255))\n    role: Mapped[str] = mapped_column(String(20), default=\"user\")   # roles stay local even with SSO"}, {"tags": ["PG", "5", "10", "17", "d3"], "code": "class Job(Base):\n    \"\"\"One row per background job: what users poll and what the sweeper repairs.\"\"\"\n    __tablename__ = \"jobs\"\n    id: Mapped[int] = mapped_column(primary_key=True)\n    kind: Mapped[str] = mapped_column(String(40))\n    target: Mapped[str] = mapped_column(String(500))\n    status: Mapped[str] = mapped_column(String(20), default=\"queued\")   # queued, running, succeeded, failed\n    progress: Mapped[int] = mapped_column(Integer, default=0)\n    error: Mapped[str | None] = mapped_column(Text)\n    requested_by: Mapped[int] = mapped_column(ForeignKey(\"users.id\"))\n    created_at: Mapped[datetime] = mapped_column(DateTime(timezone=True), server_default=func.now())\n    started_at: Mapped[datetime | None] = mapped_column(DateTime(timezone=True))\n    heartbeat_at: Mapped[datetime | None] = mapped_column(DateTime(timezone=True))\n    finished_at: Mapped[datetime | None] = mapped_column(DateTime(timezone=True))"}, {"tags": ["PG", "FILES", "10", "13", "d3", "d4"], "code": "class Result(Base):\n    \"\"\"What the job produces. The unique key is what makes a retried job safe: it upserts.\"\"\"\n    __tablename__ = \"results\"\n    __table_args__ = (UniqueConstraint(\"job_id\", \"item_key\"),)\n    id: Mapped[int] = mapped_column(primary_key=True)\n    job_id: Mapped[int] = mapped_column(ForeignKey(\"jobs.id\", ondelete=\"CASCADE\"))\n    item_key: Mapped[str] = mapped_column(String(500))\n    payload: Mapped[dict] = mapped_column(JSONB)\n    file_path: Mapped[str | None] = mapped_column(String(500))     # the binary lives on the files volume"}]}, {"path": "api/app/auth.py", "desc": "Authentication and authorization happen inside the API, on every request.", "regions": [{"tags": ["API", "d2"], "code": "from argon2 import PasswordHasher\nfrom argon2.exceptions import VerifyMismatchError\nfrom fastapi import Depends, HTTPException, Request, Response\nfrom itsdangerous import BadSignature, URLSafeTimedSerializer\nfrom sqlalchemy.ext.asyncio import AsyncSession\nfrom .db import get_session\nfrom .models import User\nfrom .settings import settings\n\nhasher = PasswordHasher()                                   # a current algorithm; never store plain passwords\nsigner = URLSafeTimedSerializer(settings.session_secret)   # signed cookie: the API stays stateless\nSESSION_TTL = 8 * 3600\n\ndef verify_password(hash_: str, password: str) -> bool:\n    try:\n        return hasher.verify(hash_, password)\n    except VerifyMismatchError:\n        return False"}, {"tags": ["API", "C", "4", "6", "d2"], "code": "def set_session(response: Response, user_id: int) -> None:\n    response.set_cookie(\"session\", signer.dumps({\"uid\": user_id}),\n                        httponly=True, secure=True, samesite=\"lax\", max_age=SESSION_TTL)\n\nasync def current_user(request: Request, session: AsyncSession = Depends(get_session)) -> User:\n    raw = request.cookies.get(\"session\")\n    if not raw:\n        raise HTTPException(401)                            # not signed in\n    try:\n        data = signer.loads(raw, max_age=SESSION_TTL)       # expired or tampered cookies fail here\n    except BadSignature:\n        raise HTTPException(401)\n    user = await session.get(User, data[\"uid\"])\n    if user is None:\n        raise HTTPException(401)\n    return user\n\ndef require_role(role: str):\n    async def check(user: User = Depends(current_user)) -> User:\n        if user.role != role:\n            raise HTTPException(403)                        # signed in, not allowed\n        return user\n    return check"}]}, {"path": "api/app/main.py", "desc": "The application boundary: validate, decide, write the status row, enqueue, answer.", "regions": [{"tags": ["API", "4"], "code": "from contextlib import asynccontextmanager\nimport httpx\nfrom arq import create_pool\nfrom arq.connections import RedisSettings\nfrom fastapi import Depends, FastAPI, HTTPException, Response\nfrom pydantic import BaseModel\nfrom sqlalchemy import select, text\nfrom sqlalchemy.ext.asyncio import AsyncSession\nfrom .auth import current_user, require_role, set_session, verify_password\nfrom .db import get_session\nfrom .models import Job, Result, User\nfrom .settings import settings"}, {"tags": ["API", "REDIS", "7"], "code": "@asynccontextmanager\nasync def lifespan(app: FastAPI):\n    app.state.arq = await create_pool(RedisSettings.from_dsn(settings.redis_url))   # one Redis pool per process\n    yield\n    await app.state.arq.close()\n\napp = FastAPI(title=\"Example API\", lifespan=lifespan)   # /docs and /openapi.json come from the routes below"}, {"tags": ["API", "C", "4", "5", "8", "d2"], "code": "class LoginIn(BaseModel):\n    email: str\n    password: str\n\n@app.post(\"/auth/login\")\nasync def login(body: LoginIn, response: Response, session: AsyncSession = Depends(get_session)):\n    user = (await session.execute(select(User).where(User.email == body.email))).scalar_one_or_none()\n    if user is None or not verify_password(user.password_hash, body.password):\n        raise HTTPException(401)\n    set_session(response, user.id)\n    return {\"ok\": True}"}, {"tags": ["API", "PG", "REDIS", "C", "4", "5", "7", "8", "d3"], "code": "class JobIn(BaseModel):\n    kind: str\n    target: str\n\n@app.post(\"/jobs\", status_code=202)\nasync def create_job(body: JobIn, user: User = Depends(current_user),\n                     session: AsyncSession = Depends(get_session)):\n    job = Job(kind=body.kind, target=body.target, requested_by=user.id)      # 1. status row first\n    session.add(job)\n    await session.commit()\n    queued = await app.state.arq.enqueue_job(\"run_job\", job.id,               # 2. enqueue with the row id\n                                             _job_id=f\"job-{job.id}\")         #    the job id prevents duplicates\n    if queued is None:                                                        #    already queued: fine\n        pass\n    return {\"id\": job.id, \"status\": job.status}                               # 3. answer at once; the client polls"}, {"tags": ["API", "PG", "C", "17", "4", "5", "d3"], "code": "@app.get(\"/jobs/{job_id}\")\nasync def job_status(job_id: int, user: User = Depends(current_user),\n                     session: AsyncSession = Depends(get_session)):\n    job = await session.get(Job, job_id)                                      # the poll reads the status row\n    if job is None:\n        raise HTTPException(404)\n    return {\"id\": job.id, \"status\": job.status, \"progress\": job.progress, \"error\": job.error}\n\n@app.get(\"/jobs/{job_id}/results\")\nasync def job_results(job_id: int, user: User = Depends(current_user),\n                      session: AsyncSession = Depends(get_session)):\n    rows = (await session.execute(select(Result).where(Result.job_id == job_id))).scalars().all()\n    return [{\"item\": r.item_key, \"data\": r.payload, \"file\": r.file_path} for r in rows]"}, {"tags": ["API", "X1", "15", "d3"], "code": "class ExplainIn(BaseModel):\n    finding: str\n\n@app.post(\"/explain\")\nasync def explain(body: ExplainIn, user: User = Depends(current_user)):\n    # A short external call a user waits for: bounded by a timeout below the proxy's 60 s.\n    async with httpx.AsyncClient(timeout=20) as client:\n        r = await client.post(\"https://llm.example.com/v1/generate\",\n                              headers={\"Authorization\": f\"Bearer {settings.llm_api_key}\"},\n                              json={\"model\": settings.llm_model, \"input\": body.finding})\n    r.raise_for_status()\n    return {\"text\": r.json()[\"output\"]}     # advisory text; not stored"}, {"tags": ["API", "PG", "REDIS", "LOGS", 